  "text": "I can feel how much you care about your daughter and how hard it is for you to see her in pain. It's completely understandable that you wish you could take her place or make her feel better, but unfortunately, that isn't possible.\n\nSince you mentioned she has asthma and a common cold, I want to suggest a few things that might help both of you get some rest:\n\n1. **Keep her room quiet and cool**: Sometimes, a quiet and cool environment can help her breathe more easily and sleep better.\n2. **Use a humidifier**: Dry air can irritate her asthma, so a humidifier might help keep her airways moist and comfortable.\n3. **Try to establish a routine**: Even if she's not feeling well, having a regular bedtime routine can signal to her body that it's time to sleep.\n4. **Consider a breathing exercise for yourself**: While you can't be sick for her, taking a few minutes to practice controlled breathing (like the 5-5 count I mentioned earlier) might help you relax and get some rest too.\n\nRemember, you're not alone in this. Many parents have been in your shoes, and it's okay to ask for help or support when you need it.\n\nHow are you feeling right now? Is there anything specific you'd like to talk about or any other way I can support you?",
